<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Lamu</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1813 CE (approx.) · capital Lamu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Swahili island town on the Kenyan coast whose well-preserved old town is the finest surviving example of Swahili urban architecture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlement in the Lamu archipelago goes back to at least the 9th century, and the present town grew in the 14th century as one of the Muslim trading towns of the Lamu archipelago. It was governed by a council of leading families rather than a strong dynasty, and for long competed with its larger neighbour, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pate-sultanate">Pate Sultanate</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Lamu's merchants exported mangrove poles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and grain and imported cloth and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Its elite built coral-stone houses with carved doors and plaster niches, and mosques with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> features. It paid tribute to the Portuguese in the 16th and 17th centuries and later to Pate, while its poets wrote in the Kiamu dialect of Swahili.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1812 or 1813 Lamu defeated Pate and its Mazrui allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shela">Battle of Shela</a>, then placed itself under the protection of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/omani-empire">Omani Empire</a>, later the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar</a>. It passed to British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenya">Kenya</a> with the coastal strip. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamu-old-town">Lamu Old Town</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lamu.jpg" alt="Lamu" width="320" height="181"><figcaption>Image: Almaddy2022 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lamu_Old_Town.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1813 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lamu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swahili</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Kenya</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Lamu, Lamu city-state</dd></dl></aside></div>

